---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import { docHref, SITE } from "../lib/site";

const UDEV_RULE =
  'SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="0bb4", ATTR{idProduct}=="0c01", TAG+="uaccess"';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Install from the browser"
  description="Install Pinna on an Echo (2nd gen) or an Echo Dot (2nd gen) from Chrome or Edge, over USB."
  slug="install"
>
  <div id="install-unsupported" class="pn-notice" data-tone="danger" hidden>
    <Icon name="close" class="pn-notice-icon" />
    <div class="pn-notice-body">
      <p class="pn-notice-title !my-0">This browser can't talk to USB devices</p>
      <p class="!my-0">
        Open this page in Chrome, Edge, Brave or Opera on a computer. Firefox
        and Safari don't support WebUSB. Or install from a terminal, as described in
        <a class="link" href={docHref("flash")}>Install Pinna</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="pn-notice mt-5" data-tone="caution">
    <Icon name="shield" class="pn-notice-icon" />
    <div class="pn-notice-body">
      <p class="pn-notice-title !my-0">For a device that was unlocked before</p>
      <p class="!my-0">
        This page installs Pinna on a device that runs Pinna or the previous
        firmware. It erases the device's data. A device that still runs
        Amazon's software needs the bootloader unlock first; this page can
        check such a device, but can't unlock it yet.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>What you need</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Chrome, Edge, Brave or Opera on a computer.</li>
    <li>
      An internet connection: the page downloads the release from
      <code>updates.getpinna.dev</code>. For a build of your own, its
      <code>pinna-boot.img</code>, <code>pinna-&lt;version&gt;.root.img</code>
      and <code>SHA256SUMS</code> instead.
    </li>
    <li>
      For the Echo Dot (2nd gen): a micro-USB cable that carries data, plugged
      straight into the computer, not into a hub.
    </li>
    <li>
      For the Echo (2nd gen): a connection to the USB pads on its base, with
      pogo pins or a soldered cable. The device has no USB port.
    </li>
    <li>
      On Linux: a udev rule that lets your user open the device. See
      <a class="link" href="#cannot-open">The browser can't open the device</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      On Windows: the WinUSB driver for the device. See
      <a class="link" href="#windows">Windows needs the WinUSB driver</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      If you want to keep what is on the device: a backup, made as described in
      <a class="link" href={`${docHref("requirements")}#backup-of-the-device`}>What you need</a>.
      This page can't make one yet.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Install</h2>

  <form id="install-form" class="not-prose" onsubmit="return false">
    <fieldset id="install-models" class="flex flex-col gap-2">
      <legend class="text-ink mb-2 font-semibold">1. Choose your device</legend>
      <label class="flex items-center gap-2">
        <input type="radio" name="model" value="echo-dot-2" required />
        Echo Dot (2nd gen)
      </label>
      <label class="flex items-center gap-2">
        <input type="radio" name="model" value="echo-2" />
        Echo (2nd gen)
      </label>
    </fieldset>

    <fieldset id="install-channels" class="mt-6 flex flex-col gap-2">
      <legend class="text-ink mb-2 font-semibold">2. Get Pinna</legend>
      <p class="text-ink-muted !mt-0 !mb-1 text-sm">
        The page downloads the newest release and checks its signature and
        each file before it writes anything.
      </p>
      <label class="flex items-center gap-2">
        <input type="radio" name="channel" value="stable" checked />
        Stable: finished releases
      </label>
      <label class="flex items-center gap-2">
        <input type="radio" name="channel" value="beta" />
        Beta: also releases before they are finished
      </label>
    </fieldset>
    <div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
      <button id="install-download" type="button" class="pn-btn">Download</button>
    </div>
    <ul id="install-download-status" class="mt-2 flex flex-col gap-1 text-sm" aria-live="polite"></ul>
    <details class="mt-3">
      <summary class="text-ink-muted cursor-pointer text-sm font-semibold">
        Use files you downloaded or built
      </summary>
      <p class="text-ink-muted mt-2 mb-2 text-sm">
        Select <code>pinna-boot.img</code>, <code>pinna-&lt;version&gt;.root.img</code> and
        <code>SHA256SUMS</code> together. The page checks each file against
        <code>SHA256SUMS</code>; the device checks the root image's signature.
      </p>
      <input id="install-files" type="file" multiple class="pn-input w-full py-2" />
      <ul id="install-files-status" class="mt-2 flex flex-col gap-1 text-sm"></ul>
    </details>
  </form>

  <p class="text-ink mt-6 mb-2 font-semibold">3. Start the device in fastboot</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Unplug the device.</li>
    <li>On an Echo (2nd gen), connect its USB pads to the computer first.</li>
    <li>Hold <strong>Volume Down (−)</strong>.</li>
    <li>
      Plug in the power while you hold it: on the Echo Dot (2nd gen) the
      micro-USB cable, with the other end on your computer; on the Echo (2nd
      gen) its power adapter.
    </li>
    <li>When the light ring changes, release the button.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    A device that still runs Amazon's software starts fastboot with
    <strong>Action</strong> instead of Volume Down. The page can check it, but not
    install on it.
  </p>

  <p class="text-ink mt-6 mb-2 font-semibold">4. Connect</p>
  <p>
    The browser asks which device to use. Choose <strong>Android</strong>
    (<code>0bb4:0c01</code>). The page then reads what the device is; it
    writes nothing yet.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
    <button id="install-connect" type="button" class="pn-btn pn-btn-primary" disabled>
      Connect
    </button>
    <p id="install-status" class="text-ink-muted !my-0 text-sm" role="status" aria-live="polite">
      Choose your device first.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div id="install-result" class="pn-notice mt-5" hidden>
    <span data-icon="ok" hidden><Icon name="check" class="pn-notice-icon" /></span>
    <span data-icon="caution" hidden><Icon name="shield" class="pn-notice-icon" /></span>
    <span data-icon="danger" hidden><Icon name="close" class="pn-notice-icon" /></span>
    <div class="pn-notice-body">
      <p id="install-headline" class="pn-notice-title !my-0"></p>
      <p id="install-detail" class="!my-0"></p>
      <p id="install-next" class="!my-0"></p>
      <dl id="install-facts" class="mt-2 grid grid-cols-[max-content_minmax(0,1fr)] gap-x-6 gap-y-1 text-sm"></dl>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section id="install-confirm" class="mt-6" hidden>
    <p class="text-ink mb-2 font-semibold">5. Install</p>
    <p class="!mt-0">The page writes, in this order:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>system_a</code>: Pinna's root filesystem.</li>
      <li><code>system_b</code>: its beginning is cleared.</li>
      <li>
        <code>userdata</code>: erased. Settings, Wi-Fi networks, the pairing with
        Home Assistant and anything else stored on the device are gone.
      </li>
      <li><code>boot_a</code> and <code>boot_b</code>: Pinna's boot image.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Keep the device connected until the page says it's done, about a minute.
      If the install stops halfway, the device stays in fastboot and you can
      start again from step 3.
    </p>
    <label class="not-prose flex items-start gap-2">
      <input id="install-agree" type="checkbox" class="mt-1" />
      <span id="install-agree-text">I want to erase this device and install Pinna on it.</span>
    </label>
    <div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
      <button id="install-start" type="button" class="pn-btn pn-btn-primary" disabled>Install Pinna</button>
      <span id="install-start-hint" class="text-ink-muted text-sm"></span>
    </div>
    <div id="install-progress-box" class="mt-4" hidden>
      <progress id="install-progress" class="w-full" max="1" value="0"></progress>
      <p id="install-progress-text" class="text-ink-muted !mt-1 text-sm" aria-live="polite"></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div id="install-done" class="pn-notice mt-6" data-tone="ok" hidden>
    <Icon name="check" class="pn-notice-icon" />
    <div class="pn-notice-body">
      <p class="pn-notice-title !my-0">Pinna is installed</p>
      <p class="!my-0">
        The device restarts now. After about 20 seconds your computer sees a USB
        device named <strong>Pinna</strong>. Open
        <a class="link" href="http://10.42.0.1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">http://10.42.0.1</a>
        for its dashboard. To connect it to Wi-Fi, follow
        <a class="link" href={`${docHref("flash")}#connect-to-wi-fi`}>Connect to Wi-Fi</a>.
        If the device is named <strong>Pinna rescue</strong> instead, see
        <a class="link" href={`${docHref("flash")}#check-the-device`}>Check the device</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div id="install-problem" class="pn-notice mt-5" data-tone="danger" hidden>
    <Icon name="close" class="pn-notice-icon" />
    <div class="pn-notice-body">
      <p id="install-problem-title" class="pn-notice-title !my-0"></p>
      <p id="install-problem-text" class="!my-0"></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section id="install-report-box" class="mt-6" hidden>
    <p class="text-ink mb-2 font-semibold">Report</p>
    <p class="!mt-0 text-sm">
      If the result looks wrong, or the page can't go on, paste this into an
      issue. The serial number is cut short.
    </p>
    <textarea
      id="install-report"
      class="pn-input is-mono h-56 w-full py-2"
      readonly
      aria-label="Device report"></textarea>
    <div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
      <button id="install-copy" type="button" class="pn-btn">Copy</button>
      <a id="install-issue" class="pn-btn" href={`${SITE.repo}/issues/new`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        Open an issue
        <Icon name="external" size={14} />
      </a>
      <span id="install-copy-status" class="text-ink-muted text-sm" aria-live="polite"></span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2>If it doesn't work</h2>

  <h3>The device isn't in the list</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The device isn't in fastboot. Start it again as described in step 3.
    </li>
    <li>
      The cable only charges. Try another cable, and a USB port on the
      computer itself.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="cannot-open">The browser can't open the device</h3>
  <p>
    On Linux the browser runs as your user, and only root may open USB
    devices that no rule names. Add this rule as
    <code>/etc/udev/rules.d/60-pinna-fastboot.rules</code>:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{UDEV_RULE}</code></pre>
  <p>Then load it:</p>
  <pre><code>sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger</code></pre>
  <p>
    On NixOS, put the rule in <code>services.udev.extraRules</code> instead.
    Then start the device in fastboot again (step 3) and connect again.
  </p>

  <h3 id="busy">Another program uses the device</h3>
  <p>
    <code>fastboot</code>, <code>adb</code> (run <code>adb kill-server</code>),
    Android Studio, or this page in another tab. Close them and connect again.
  </p>

  <h3 id="windows">Windows needs the WinUSB driver</h3>
  <p>
    Install it with <a class="link" href="https://zadig.akeo.ie/">Zadig</a>
    while the device is in fastboot: choose <strong>Options › List All Devices</strong>,
    select <strong>Android</strong> (USB ID <code>0BB4 0C01</code>), choose
    <strong>WinUSB</strong> and <strong>Install Driver</strong>. Then connect again.
  </p>

  <h3 id="no-reply">The device doesn't answer, or goes away</h3>
  <p>
    The page stops after the first request that gets no reply, and sends
    nothing more. Some devices that were never unlocked reset when they get a
    request and come back in fastboot. Unplug the device, start it in fastboot
    again, use another cable or USB port, and connect again. If it happens
    every time, open an issue with the report the page shows.
  </p>

  <h3>Leave fastboot without installing</h3>
  <p>Unplug the device and plug it in again. It starts as before.</p>
</DocsLayout>

<script>
  import { startInstallPage } from "../lib/installer/install-page";
  startInstallPage();
</script>
